## Task 9B3 bounded policy callbacks

Commit `8868e96790cb0fb71975df85a76e9a6c7e7114a9` (`fix(pit-v5): bound selector callbacks`)
closes higher-order callback and sequence-allocation escape paths in the V5 candidate language.
Only `min`/`max` may accept a callback, it must be a direct private one-argument policy helper,
and its input must be either an exact authenticated V3 collection path or a bounded tuple.
Callback execution is charged by the authenticated collection bound in the same acyclic static
call graph as direct helper calls. The validator uses an iterative graph proof with exact call-depth
and work ceilings, so adversarially deep source cannot recurse inside the controller validator.

Dynamic string/bytes/tuple concatenation, percent formatting, f-strings, and sequence repetition
are rejected. Numeric `Add` and `Mod` remain usable through a narrow syntax-only numeric proof;
fixed immutable tuple literals may be concatenated only when their combined item count remains
bounded. The proof tracks local assignments conservatively and refuses rebinding ambiguity. This
is deliberately not a general Python type system: snapshot values must be explicitly converted by
approved numeric builtins, while authenticated portfolio/eviction collections retain bounded
selector and reducer traversal.

The focused verification commands and results were:

- `python -B -m py_compile core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` — passed.
- `python -m ruff check core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` — `All checks passed!`.
- `git diff --check -- core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` — passed (only the existing Windows
  LF-to-CRLF advisory was emitted).
- The current-policy fake reported `AST_CURRENT_POLICIES_OK accepted=4`.
- The bounded selector fake reported `AST_SAFE_SELECTOR_OK selectors=2 numeric_add_mod=accepted`.
- The bypass corpus fake reported `AST_REJECTION_CORPUS_OK rejected=21`.
- The graph-bound fake reported
  `AST_GRAPH_BOUNDS_OK callback_work=rejected callback_depth=rejected local_rebind=rejected collection_authority=rejected`.
- The long-graph fake reported
  `AST_LONG_GRAPH_OK depth=200 rejected_without_validator_recursion`.
- The focused commit's pre-commit Ruff, whitespace, end-of-file, YAML, and merge-conflict hooks all
  passed.

The cached diff contained only `core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` (381 insertions and 115
deletions). No provider, Docker, Git materialization, market-data, network, replay, or test-suite
operation ran for this correction, and no test file was created or modified. The pre-existing
production factory/bootstrap edits remained unstaged and were preserved unchanged.

## Task 9B3 trusted evaluator image packaging

Commit `75c7fc2d34c4be6afb8f2d211e7c3dce3629ce4d` (`fix(pit-v5): package trusted evaluator image`)
adds a dedicated digest-pinned V5 evaluator/probe image definition and its Dockerfile-specific
deny-all build-context allowlist. The allowlist contains exactly the 53 authenticated project-source
paths in `EVALUATOR_SOURCE_PATHS_V5`, plus the Dockerfile, its ignore file, and the dependency lock.
It excludes all four editable V3 policy files, PIT data, local artifacts, tests, environment files,
secrets, and provider/controller sources. The trusted source closure is installed root-owned under
the interpreter site-packages path; both probe and panel commands use `python -P` so a mounted source
working directory cannot shadow image-owned modules.

The V5 AST parser no longer imports the capability-heavy legacy optimizer module. Six missing
top-level evaluator dependencies are now part of the authenticated source map. The evaluator
contract source digest must exactly equal the sandbox profile runtime-source digest at authority,
request, and concrete-runtime construction boundaries. The image carries only two exact PIT V5
labels, and concrete image inspection rejects missing, extra, or mismatched labels before container
creation. The immutable image reference remains repository-name plus digest; the image digest covers
the base runtime and installed dependencies while the runtime-source label binds the exact project
source map that the Dockerfile installs.

