## Task 9B3 rounded-value allocation fix

Commit `643ff90651c59ad6435f840d399f2be5f5a05170` (`fix(pit-v5): classify rounded
values safely`) closes the reviewed sequence-allocation bypass by conservatively classifying every
allowed `round(...)` call as integer-valued. This matches the one-argument and explicit-`None`
forms and prevents rounded values from serving as the falsely inferred floating operand that had
allowed dynamic sequence repetition. A policy can still request a floating proof explicitly with
`float(round(...))`; ordinary numeric round, addition, modulo, and casts remain usable.

The focused verification commands and direct-fake results were:

- `python -B -m py_compile core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` — passed.
- `python -m ruff check core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` — `All checks passed!`.
- `git diff --check -- core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` — passed (only the existing Windows
  LF-to-CRLF advisory was emitted).
- The direct fake reported `PIT_V5_ROUND_KIND_OK policies=4 bypasses=rejected
  numeric_round=accepted explicit_float_cast=accepted numeric_mod=accepted`; it validated all four
  current V3 policy files, rejected both `"x" * round(float(1000000000000))` and
  `"x" * round(float(1000000000000), None)`, and accepted the intended numeric forms.
- The focused commit's pre-commit Ruff, whitespace, end-of-file, YAML, and merge-conflict hooks all
  passed.

The cached diff contained only `core/pit_optimizer_v5/candidate_ir.py` (two insertions and two
deletions). No provider, Docker/container, image-build, network, data, replay, or test-suite
operation ran, and no test file was created or modified. The pre-existing production
factory/bootstrap edits remained unstaged and were restored unchanged after the commit hooks.

## Task 9B3 trusted panel runtime and image attestation

Commit `350e5e84a5493a72e8119418f55c308a78429f60` (`fix(pit-v5): complete trusted
panel runtime`) adds the real image-resident quick/discovery panel entrypoint and its closed
canonical protocol. The host authenticates the complete referenced `EvaluationPanelSpec`, persists
one request-bound input record, pins that exact regular file with the four candidate-policy files
and two PIT data files, and mounts only those seven read-only files plus the bounded output
directory. The image-owned entrypoint reauthenticates the request, policy source, PIT bundle, and
provenance before invoking `PitPanelEvaluatorV5`; its canonical output is bound to both the outer
Docker request and the exact mounted input digest. Panel and semantic-probe execution now share the
same trusted image-owned module path and `/` working directory, so candidate files cannot shadow
trusted evaluator modules.

The dedicated image allowlist now contains all 56 authenticated evaluator source paths, including
the panel entry, protocol, and build verifier, while continuing to exclude the four editable policy
files and all PIT data, artifacts, tests, provider/controller source, and secrets. During image
construction, `core.pit_optimizer_v5.image_manifest` recomputes the canonical source-map digest
from the installed allowlisted bytes and fails unless it equals `PIT_V5_RUNTIME_SOURCE_SHA256`;
only then is the same value written to the exact runtime-source label.

